    {
      "id": "cmsfkfuy5000104jozt2jbmvc",
      "term": "Walk me through the 3 financial statements & how they generally work",
      "setTitle": "Accounting - \"Talking\"",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "The three financial statements are the income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow statement, and each answers a different question about the business",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The income statement measures profitability over a period, flowing from revenue down through costs to net income",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Net income tells you whether the company made or lost money",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The balance sheet is a snapshot at a point in time showing assets — resources like cash, inventory, and PP&E",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Assets are funded either by liabilities like debt and payables or by shareholder equity, and assets always equal liabilities plus equity",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "The cash flow statement measures liquidity — actual cash generated and used over a period",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The cash flow statement starts with net income, adds back non-cash charges like D&A, and adjusts for working capital to get operating cash flow",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Investing activities capture cash spent on long-term assets like CapEx, and financing activities capture debt, equity, and dividend flows",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Net income from the income statement feeds the cash flow statement and flows into retained earnings on the balance sheet, and the cash flow statement's net change in cash explains why the balance sheet's cash balance changed",
          "kind": "causal"
        }
      ],
